dict(slug="market-reset-without-central-actor", section="Market Intelligence", date="2026-11-10",
title="How a Market Reset Can Happen Without a Central Actor",
desc="Episode 1: why Tulum's real story is the shift in bargaining power, as softer tourism, selective credit and cash flow separate comparable assets.",
body="""One market. One headline. Wrong conclusion.

Tulum is entering a phase where the more important story may not be whether the market is "up" or "down," but how bargaining power is changing inside it.

Tourism demand has softened. Credit is becoming more selective. Cash flow matters more. And assets that once appeared comparable are beginning to separate by legal quality, capital structure and resilience.

That does not automatically mean collapse. And it does not require a central actor directing the outcome.

> A market can reset itself when independent forces begin reacting to the same pressure at the same time.

## Why it matters for cross-border capital

Patient capital can gain negotiating power before the broader market recognizes what is happening. For buyers evaluating Riviera Maya real estate from Chicago, Toronto or across the Great Lakes, this distinction matters more than the headline.

Source framework: SITURQ, Municipality of Tulum, banking-market data and international real-estate precedents.""",
series="Episode 1 of 3 · The Tulum Reset"),

dict(slug="how-selective-credit-reprices-the-market", section="Market Intelligence", date="2026-11-12",
title="How Selective Credit Reprices the Market",
desc="Episode 2: why bridge lenders' selectivity separates developments on the same street, and why cash and clean documentation now carry a premium in Tulum.",
body="""The asking price is not always where a real-estate market reprices first. Sometimes it happens inside a credit committee.

When bridge lenders become more selective, two developments on the same street can suddenly become very different investments. One sponsor can finance completion, carry inventory and wait. Another needs continuous pre-sales, refinancing or fresh equity simply to remain on schedule.

That difference eventually reaches price.

## What the data shows

Current banking data from Quintana Roo points to exactly this kind of transition: higher delinquency, lower bank-credit placement and a specific pullback from construction and mortgage lending in Tulum after several years of rapid residential expansion.

## The questions that follow

- Who still gets financed?
- Who has to restructure?
- Why can cash, balance-sheet strength and clean documentation become more valuable than launch momentum?

> For private capital evaluating Mexico, understanding the financing behind the asset may now be as important as understanding the asset itself.""",
series="Episode 2 of 3 · The Tulum Reset"),

dict(slug="distressed-or-impaired", section="Market Intelligence", date="2026-11-14",
title="Distressed or Impaired? Where the Real Opportunity Is",
desc="Episode 3: six filters to separate a temporarily distressed Tulum asset from a structurally impaired one before you buy at a discount.",
body="""A discount is not the same thing as an opportunity. Sometimes it is simply a cheaper way to acquire a permanent problem.

The distinction becomes critical when a real-estate market enters a period of selective credit and uneven liquidity.

## Curable problems

- A seller may need cash.
- A bridge loan may be approaching maturity.
- A developer may need equity to complete construction.

## Problems capital does not cure

Unclear title, unresolved fiduciary claims, defective permits, weak structural demand or impaired exit liquidity are different. Capital does not automatically cure them.

## Six filters

- Legal quality.
- Capital structure.
- Completion risk.
- Micromarket demand.
- Price versus replacement cost.
- Exit liquidity.

> The opportunity is not simply to buy Tulum at a lower price. It is to identify temporary dislocation before the market distinguishes it from structural risk.""",
series="Episode 3 of 3 · The Tulum Reset"),

